### Describe the bug

When a property carries `[Obsolete]` and its type is componentized (lifted into `#/components/schemas`), `deprecated: true` is written into the **shared component** as well as onto the `$ref` use site.

One `[Obsolete]` property therefore marks the type as deprecated for every other consumer of that type across the whole document, including unrelated endpoints.

This looks like a regression in 11.0. `System.Text.Json`'s `JsonSchemaExporter` began emitting `deprecated` itself in https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/130665 (milestone 11.0-preview7). `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` already had its own handling for `[Obsolete]`, which routes the flag to the use site via the `x-ref-deprecated` annotation. Both now fire, and the inline keyword STJ wrote is never removed when the schema is componentized.

`[Description]` is unaffected, because STJ does not read `[Description]` — only ASP.NET Core does, and it routes it correctly.

### Expected Behavior

`deprecated` should appear only at the use site, matching `description`:

```json
"components": {
  "schemas": {
    "Address": {
      "required": [ "city" ],
      "type": "object",
      "properties": { "city": { "type": "string" } }
    },
    "CreateOrderRequest": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "billingAddress": {
          "description": "No longer used. Billing addresses now come from the customer profile.",
          "deprecated": true,
          "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Address"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Steps To Reproduce

A .NET file-based app — no project file, no local build, runs against the published package. Save as `deprecated.cs` and run `dotnet run deprecated.cs`:

```csharp
#:sdk Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web
#:package Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi@11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128
#:property JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=true

using System.ComponentModel;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi;
using Microsoft.OpenApi;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Logging.ClearProviders();
builder.Services.AddOpenApi();
var app = builder.Build();

// Two unrelated endpoints. Both use the shared `Address` type.
// Only CreateOrderRequest deprecates its address property.
app.MapPost("/orders", (CreateOrderRequest r) => { });
app.MapPost("/shipments", (CreateShipmentRequest r) => { });

await app.StartAsync();
var provider = app.Services.GetRequiredKeyedService<IOpenApiDocumentProvider>("v1");
var document = await provider.GetOpenApiDocumentAsync();
Console.WriteLine(await document.SerializeAsJsonAsync(OpenApiSpecVersion.OpenApi3_1));
await app.StopAsync();

public sealed class CreateOrderRequest
{
    [Description("No longer used. Billing addresses now come from the customer profile.")]
    [Obsolete]
    public Address BillingAddress { get; set; } = new() { City = "Amsterdam" };
}

public sealed class CreateShipmentRequest
{
    // Nothing deprecated here, and nothing deprecated on Address itself.
    public Address DeliveryAddress { get; set; } = new() { City = "Amsterdam" };
}

public sealed class Address
{
    public required string City { get; set; }
}
```

Actual `components.schemas` (OpenAPI 3.1):

```json
{
  "Address": {
    "required": [ "city" ],
    "type": "object",
    "properties": { "city": { "type": "string" } },
    "deprecated": true
  },
  "CreateOrderRequest": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "billingAddress": {
        "description": "No longer used. Billing addresses now come from the customer profile.",
        "deprecated": true,
        "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Address"
      }
    }
  },
  "CreateShipmentRequest": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "deliveryAddress": { "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Address" }
    }
  }
}
```

`Address` is marked deprecated even though neither the class nor `CreateShipmentRequest.DeliveryAddress` is obsolete. `/shipments` is collateral damage from a `[Obsolete]` on an unrelated endpoint's property.

Note: `JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault=true` is needed only because file-based apps default it off, which leaves the source-generated `OpenApiJsonSchemaContext` as the sole resolver and breaks body binding. It is unrelated to this bug.

### Exceptions (if any)

None — the document generates successfully, it is just incorrect.

### .NET Version

11.0.100-rc.1.26420.103

### Anything else?

**Root cause**

`JsonSchemaExporter` now stamps `"deprecated": true` onto the property's schema node before `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` ever sees it. Dumping the raw `JsonNode` at the end of `OpenApiSchemaService.TransformSchemaNode` for the repro above:

```
type=Address prop=billingAddress willBeComponentized=True
  {"type":"object","properties":{"city":{"type":"string"}},"required":["city"],
   "deprecated":true,                      <-- written by System.Text.Json
   "x-schema-id":"Address",
   "x-ref-description":"No longer used. ...",
   "x-ref-deprecated":true}                <-- written by ASP.NET Core, correct
```

Key insertion order confirms the origin: `deprecated` precedes `x-schema-id`, which `ApplySchemaReferenceId` adds near the top of `TransformSchemaNode`.

Neither ASP.NET Core branch wrote the inline keyword:

- The type-level check is not the source. Instrumenting it prints `underlyingType=Address attrs=[NullableContextAttribute,NullableAttribute,RequiredMemberAttribute] obsolete=False`.
- The property-level inline branch did not run either — `willBeComponentized=True`, so `isInlinedSchema` is false and the `else` branch correctly wrote `x-ref-deprecated`.

Confirmed directly against System.Text.Json with no ASP.NET Core involved:

```csharp
var opts = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web)
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()
};

opts.GetJsonSchemaAsNode(typeof(CreateOrderRequest)).ToJsonString();
// {"type":["object","null"],"properties":{"billingAddress":{
//    "type":"object","properties":{"city":{"type":"string"}},
//    "required":["city"],"deprecated":true}}}

// the same type with only [Description] on the property, no [Obsolete]:
// ...no "deprecated" key
```

So when the schema is componentized, ASP.NET Core needs to remove the inline `deprecated` keyword that STJ wrote, in addition to setting `x-ref-deprecated`.

**The behaviour is order-dependent**

Whether the leak is visible depends on which property first causes the component to be registered, because the component is built from that first node:

| Case | Container shape | `Address` component |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | single `[Obsolete]` nullable property | `deprecated: true` — leaks |
| B | single `[Obsolete]` non-nullable property | `deprecated: true` — leaks |
| C | a clean property declared **first**, then the `[Obsolete]` one | clean — no leak |
| D | single `[Description]` property, no `[Obsolete]` | clean — no leak |
| E | `[Obsolete]` property and clean property in **separate endpoints** | `deprecated: true` — leaks across endpoints |

Case C explains why the existing tests do not catch this. `src/OpenApi/sample/Endpoints/MapObsoleteEndpoints.cs` declares the clean `CurrentReference` before the `[Obsolete] LegacyReference`, so the committed snapshot happens to look correct. Swapping the declaration order should change the generated document.

**Related**

- Property-level `[Obsolete]` support was added in #63494.
- #58617 established the same principle from the other direction: schema-level facts belong on the component, use-site facts do not.
- #69727 is a separate placement bug for the same annotations, in the nullable + componentized case.
- https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/131631 proposes that `JsonSchemaExporter` also emit `readOnly`/`writeOnly`. If that lands, the identical double-write will occur for those keywords unless this is fixed structurally rather than for `deprecated` alone.

**Environment**

- Reproduced against the published `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` `11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128`, and against `main` with a local build.
- Not verified directly on 10.0 — no 10.0 runtime was installed here. The regression claim rests on dotnet/runtime#130665 having shipped in 11.0-preview7. Anyone with the 10.0 runtime can check by changing the repro to `#:package Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi@10.0.12` plus `#:property TargetFramework=net10.0`.

